DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

## randomize smallest and largest (with no effect on median)
$smallest = 37 - random(0, 10);
$largest  = 46 + random(0, 10);

#  Setup table
our @salaries = (41, 38, 39, 45, $largest, 42, 44, 41, $smallest, 42);

$rb = RadioButtons([ [ 'histogram', 'bar graph', 'pie chart', 'box plot' ] ],
    0, labels => 'ABC');

BEGIN_PGML
The starting salaries (in $1000) for ten UBC graduates are given below.

[#
        [.41.][.38.][.39.][.45.][.[$largest.][.42.][.44.][.41.][.[$smallest.][.42.]
#]{align=>'|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|', horizontalrules =>1, padding =>0.3}
  
  
You are interested in finding out the *shape* of the salary distribution. Which of the following display methods would be *best* for your purpose?
[_]{$rb}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
In general, a *histogram* shows a distribution of number in the best way.
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
